ce may be negligible. However we already have heap code working in previous correct integrations.\n\nGiven that our current source uses heaps, we can keep that and add flattening from C. But flattening would require changing the parallel loop structure significantly: instead of iterating over groups, iterate over flattened index. We'll need to precompute total_points and allocate array of (label, x, y) for each point.\n\nAlternatively, we could stick with group-level parallelism but fix the local_size bug and ensure correctness. That might be simpler and less error-prone.\n\nGiven time constraints, I think fixing the current source's bugs is most reliable. The previous parent failed due to \"Protected correctness checks failed\" which likely means output mismatch for some test case. Our current source has a clear bug: local_size reset per group. Also note that in heap_insert_or_replace, when hs < k we do insertion and bubble up; but if the heap already contains sentinel values (FLT_MAX), then new distances will be smaller than root? Actually initial sentinel is FLT_MAX, so first insertions will replace them correctly. However after filling to k, subsequent points with larger distance than root are skipped. That's correct.\n\nBut there might also be an issue: In the merge phase, we iterate over all threads and for each local heap from index 1 to sz, call heap_insert_or_replace on global_heap. This works but note that local heaps may not be sorted; they are max-heaps so root is largest. Inserting them one by one into another max-heap will produce correct final set of k smallest distances? Actually we want the overall k smallest distances across all threads. Since each thread's heap contains its own top-k (smallest), merging them via a global max-heap works: for each element from local heaps, insert/replace in global heap; after processing all, global heap will contain the overall k smallest. That is correct.\n\nPotential issue: The initial sentinel values FLT_MAX may cause problems if actual distances are also large? But they should be finite. Also note that we set labels to -1 for sentinels; frequency counting ignores negative labels (if(labels[i] >= 0)). So fine.\n\nNow, the tie-breaking in frequency counting: We iterate c from 1 to 255 and update only if freq[c] > best_count. This picks smallest ASCII with max count. Original baseline uses qsort then linear scan which also picks first occurrence in sorted order (ascending). Since we are not sorting labels but using counts, the result should be identical because for equal frequencies, both methods pick the same label? Let's test: Suppose labels = {'B','A'} and k=2; original sorts to ['A','B'], then scan: most_frequent='A', count=1; next 'B' has freq 1 not >1 so stays 'A'. Our frequency array: freq['A']=1, freq['B']=1; best_count starts at freq[labels[0]] which is labels[0]? Actually we set best_label = labels[0]; but labels[0] could be any label from heap (not sorted). That's a problem! In our code:\n\nchar best_label = labels[0];\nint best_count = freq[(unsigned char)best_label];\nfor (int c = 1; c < 256; c++) {\n    if (freq[c] > best_count) { ... }\n}\n\nIf there are ties, we pick the first label that appears in our unsorted labels array? Actually we start with best_label = labels[0], then iterate over all possible chars. If another char has same frequency but higher ASCII, it won't replace because condition is > not >=. So if multiple chars have max count, we keep whichever was encountered first as best_label (which could be any label from the heap). This may differ from original which picks smallest ASCII among those with max count.\n\nExample: labels = {'B','A'} in some order; freq['A']=1, freq['B']=1. If labels[0] is 'B', then best_count=1, best_label='B'. Loop over c: for c='A' (65), freq[65]=1 not >1 so skip; for c='B' (66) also 1 not >1. So result is 'B'. Original would give 'A'. So mismatch!\n\nThus we must ensure tie-breaking matches original: pick smallest ASCII character among those with maximum frequency.\n\nFix: After computing frequencies, find the max count first, then iterate over all chars and update best_label when freq[c] == max_count? Actually we need to pick smallest c with that max. We can do:\n\nint max_freq = 0;\nfor (int c=0;c<256;c++) if(freq[c]>max_freq) max_freq=freq[c];\nchar best_label = '?';\nfor (int c=0;c<256;c++) if(freq[c]==max_freq){best_label=(char)c;break;}\nreturn best_label;\n\nThat matches original because qsort sorts ascending, then linear scan picks first with highest frequency.\n\nAlternatively, we could sort the labels array before counting frequencies? But that would add overhead. Better to fix tie-breaking.\n\nNow also note: In our current code, after merging into global_heap, we extract labels from positions 1..actual_k of global_heap (which is a max-heap). The order of these labels in the heap does not correspond to sorted distances; but for frequency counting it doesn't matter as long as we have correct set. However tie-breaking issue remains.\n\nSo we need to modify knn function to:\n- Fix per-thread local_size persistence across groups.\n- Fix tie-breaking in final label selection.\n\nAdditionally, we should consider using the flattening approach from C? It might improve load balance but adds complexity. Given that previous successful integrations (like 0854f1db0f074d46) used group-level parallelism and achieved ~0.029s, which is already very fast compared to baseline 2.09s (speedup ~72x). So further optimization may not be necessary; correctness is paramount.\n\nThus I will produce a corrected version of the current source with these fixes, while still incorporating all three ideas: max-heap from A, SoA and static inline distance from B, and per-thread local heaps allocated once outside parallel region (from C) plus early skip inherent in heap. Also we can add flattening? Actually idea C's \"Flatten group-point iteration into single loop over all points\" is a specific transformatio