Inside loop, compute distance using NEON batches? But points are stored in flat array, not grouped by group. We can still process 4 at a time if we have contiguous memory; but the flat array is contiguous so yes.\n- After parallel region, merge per-thread heaps into global heap as before.\n- Then frequency counting with correct tie-breaking.\n\nThis approach incorporates flattening from C and max-heap from A, SoA storage? Actually the flat array stores x,y directly, not via xs/ys arrays. But we still need to parse groups into SoA format for parsing? The Group struct currently has xs/ys; but after flattening we don't use them in knn except for building flat array. We can keep SoA for parsing and then build flat array from it.\n\nAlternatively, we could avoid flattening and just fix the bugs. Given that previous correct integrations (like 0854f1db0f074d46) used group-level parallelism with persistent per-thread heaps? Let's check its diff: It had #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static) over groups? Actually looking at interaction sources [\"11cd5b5226c14303\", \"d19340a2f7504842\", \"89c5107aac544c1f\"] - that combination produced correct program. The source 89c5107aac544c1f is the flattening one, so likely it used flattening. So to replicate success, we should use flattening.\n\nBut note: Our current source (from parent) does not have flattening; it has group-level parallelism. That parent failed correctness. So maybe the bug was due to local_size reset and tie-breaking. If we fix those without changing structure, could it become correct? Possibly yes. But to be safe and fully integrate C's idea, I'll implement flattening.\n\nLet me design the new knn function:\n\n1. Count total_points.\n2. Allocate flat array: typedef struct { char label; float x; float y; } FlatPoint; FlatPoint *all = malloc(total_points * sizeof(FlatPoint));\n3. Fill it by iterating groups and copying xs/ys/label.\n4. Determine max_threads, allocate per-thread heaps (size k+1) initialized with F